---
/**
 * The index of ideas: one row per thing people add to a portfolio, a verdict word, one
 * line, and a link to the page that argues it. The rows are data so the table and its
 * phone rendering come from one list.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import type { Confidence } from "~/lib/rungs";

interface Row {
  readonly idea: string;
  readonly verdict: Confidence;
  readonly line: string;
  readonly href: string;
  readonly more: string;
}

const ROWS: readonly Row[] = [
  {
    idea: "Cheaper funds",
    verdict: "Settled",
    line: "Index funds cost 0.09% a year against 0.57% for the average active fund, and 90% of active US stock funds lost to their benchmark over ten years.",
    href: "/strategies/fees-and-taxes/",
    more: "Fees and taxes",
  },
  {
    idea: "The right account",
    verdict: "Settled",
    line: "Which fund sits in your Roth, your IRA and your taxable account is worth a few hundredths of a point a year, every year, for the cost of a form.",
    href: "/tools/which-account/",
    more: "Which account",
  },
  {
    idea: "An ETF instead of a mutual fund",
    verdict: "Settled",
    line: "In 2025, 7% of ETFs paid out a taxable capital gain against 52% of mutual funds.",
    href: "/strategies/fees-and-taxes/",
    more: "Fees and taxes",
  },
  {
    idea: "Never trading",
    verdict: "Settled",
    line: "Every sale in a taxable account brings a tax bill forward. Not selling is the one free edge.",
    href: "/strategies/fees-and-taxes/",
    more: "Fees and taxes",
  },
  {
    idea: "Leaning toward cheaper companies",
    verdict: "Probably",
    line: "Beat a plain index mix by about 0.8 points a year between 1990 and 2026, and by 0.9 points on data from the 1980s the funds were not built on. Needs thirty years of patience.",
    href: "/strategies/value-and-momentum/",
    more: "Value and momentum",
  },
  {
    idea: "Holding foreign stocks",
    verdict: "Probably",
    line: "Worth holding because foreign markets are priced at a big discount to the US, and because nobody knows which region wins the next twenty years. Not because it protects you in a crash.",
    href: "/strategies/international/",
    more: "International",
  },
  {
    idea: "Trend following, on top of stocks",
    verdict: "Too close to call",
    line: "The strongest candidate on this site. Added 2 points a year over 96 years, most of it before 2009, and the fund that does it is under three years old.",
    href: "/strategies/trend-following/",
    more: "Trend following",
  },
  {
    idea: "The 200-day rule",
    verdict: "No",
    line: "Cuts the worst fall from 84% to 43% and pays for it with 0.7 points a year less than holding, 58 losing exits out of 73, and a tax bill.",
    href: "/strategies/market-timing/",
    more: "Market timing",
  },
  {
    idea: "2x and 3x funds",
    verdict: "No",
    line: "A 3x fund held since 1926 spent 28 years under water and fell 99.9% at its worst. Under the 200-day rule it still fell 85%.",
    href: "/strategies/market-timing/",
    more: "Market timing",
  },
  {
    idea: "Gold",
    verdict: "Too close to call",
    line: "Earned 1.75% a year over cash since 1975 and rose in 59% of the worst stock months since 2000. Small, optional, and instead of cash rather than instead of stocks.",
    href: "/strategies/gold-and-bitcoin/",
    more: "Gold and bitcoin",
  },
  {
    idea: "Bitcoin",
    verdict: "No",
    line: "The only holding tested that made the portfolio's worst fall deeper at every weight. A bet, not protection. Zero to 2% if you want the bet.",
    href: "/strategies/gold-and-bitcoin/",
    more: "Gold and bitcoin",
  },
  {
    idea: "Crash insurance and buffer funds",
    verdict: "No",
    line: "The best-known tail fund lost 8% a year while stocks made 14%, and fell 13% in the 2022 bear market. 86 of 102 buffer funds returned less than a plain stock and cash mix.",
    href: "/strategies/crash-insurance/",
    more: "Crash insurance",
  },
  {
    idea: "Bonds and TIPS",
    verdict: "Too close to call",
    line: "Owning fewer stocks is a choice about how much you can lose, not a way to earn more. Ten-year TIPS, Treasury bonds whose payments rise with inflation, now pay 2.4% above inflation, the most since 2008.",
    href: "/strategies/crash-insurance/",
    more: "Crash insurance",
  },
  {
    idea: "Carry",
    verdict: "Too close to call",
    line: "A second strategy run with futures contracts, which moves independently of trend. Positive on paper, negative since 2013, and the fund is two years old.",
    href: "/strategies/trend-following/",
    more: "Trend following",
  },
  {
    idea: "Direct indexing",
    verdict: "No",
    line: "Cost money on every one of 400 simulated thirty-year paths. Only 0.2% of the losses it harvests ever save tax for someone who never sells.",
    href: "/strategies/fees-and-taxes/",
    more: "Fees and taxes",
  },
  {
    idea: "Rebalancing",
    verdict: "Settled",
    line: "Keeps your mix from drifting. Does not earn a return, and every rule tested on a three-region mix lost a little to never trading.",
    href: "/strategies/fees-and-taxes/",
    more: "Fees and taxes",
  },
  {
    idea: "Dividend and property funds",
    verdict: "No",
    line: "A dividend fund is the stock market with a screen and a tax bill. Property funds fell 41% in 2020 and rose in 3% of the worst stock months.",
    href: "/strategies/crash-insurance/",
    more: "Crash insurance",
  },
];

const COLUMNS = ["Idea", "Verdict", "In one line", "More"] as const;
---

<Base
  title="Strategies"
  description="Seventeen things people add to a portfolio, and whether each one earns its place once you look at the whole portfolio rather than the idea on its own."
  asOf="2026-09-02"
  wide
>
  <Hero
    title="What earns a place in a portfolio"
    lede="Most things people bolt onto a portfolio look good on their own and do nothing once they are inside one. A few look unimpressive alone and help the whole. The only test that matters is the whole portfolio."
  />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The idea behind this site is simple. No single strategy beats the market by much, and none does it
    reliably. But if you can find several ideas that each have a modest edge, and they do not all fail at the
    same time, stacking them gives you better odds than any one of them. The catch is that most candidates fail
    the second half of that sentence. They either add nothing once fees and taxes are counted, or they fall
    exactly when your stocks fall, or they only help if you buy them a particular way.</p>

    <p>So every idea below was tested the same way: put it inside a whole portfolio, charge its costs, and
    compare the result with a cheap index fund holding the same mix. The verdict words are the four I use
    everywhere on this site. Settled means the direction is not in doubt. Probably means the evidence points
    one way but a long stretch of bad luck could hide it for decades. Too close to call means the test could
    not separate the idea from zero. No means it lost, on measured cost against measured benefit.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout scroll heading="The verdicts" class="mt-10">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base sm:min-w-[40rem]">
      <caption class="sr-only">Seventeen ideas, each with a verdict word, a one-line reason and a link to the page that argues it</caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          {COLUMNS.map((heading) => (
            <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4">{heading}</th>
          ))}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-muted">
        {ROWS.map((row) => (
          <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule last:border-0 align-top">
            <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 text-left font-medium text-ink">{row.idea}</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[1]} class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap"><Verdict word={row.verdict} /></td>
            <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[2]} class="py-3 pr-4 max-w-[34rem]">{row.line}</td>
            <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[3]} class="py-3 pr-4 whitespace-nowrap"><a href={row.href} class="link">{row.more}</a></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>Alone and together are different questions</h2>

    <p>Some ideas fail alone and help in a portfolio. Trend following is the clearest case. On its own it earned
    about 5% a year since 2000 by the SG Trend index's count, with long dead stretches. Bought by selling
    stocks, it dilutes the portfolio. Added on top of stocks through a fund that borrows inside itself, it rose
    in 64% of the worst stock months since 2000 and turned the lost decade of 1999 to 2009 from a loss into
    roughly zero. Cash is the other case. Cash earns the least of anything, and moving 10% of a stock
    portfolio into Treasury bills would have added 0.9% in the average terrible month since 1926, as much as
    gold or long bonds and for no fee.</p>

    <p>Others look strong alone and fail inside a portfolio. Bitcoin has the best return for its risk of
    anything I measured, and it still deepened the portfolio's worst fall at every weight because it falls
    with stocks and falls further. A standalone managed-futures fund, a trend-following fund, bought by selling stocks gives up the
    stock return on the money you sold, which is most of what the strategy was supposed to add. Gold has the
    same problem: bought by selling stocks it costs the portfolio 0.4 points a year against holding the
    stocks, added on top it gains 0.2, and neither number can be told from zero.</p>

    <p>Ask two questions of any idea. Does it fall when stocks fall? And how am I paying for it: by selling
    stocks, or by adding it on top?</p>
  </div>
</Base>
